<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two variants, one structure:</strong> the modal side sheet is
        a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> (same platform roots as the dialog and
        bottom sheet: focus trap, inert background, Escape, top layer); the
        standard is any block element with <code>fl-sidesheet--standard</code>.
        Both share surface/header/body slots, so content moves between them
        unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>Modal lifecycle:</strong> <code>data-fl-open="id"</code>
        opens it, <code>data-fl-close</code> in the header closes it, scrim
        tap closes it (a click on the transparent dialog element <em>is</em>
        the scrim click), Escape closes it, focus returns to the invoker on
        every path. The page is scroll-locked while open.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard lifecycle:</strong> visibility is author logic —
        toggle the <code>hidden</code> attribute (the demo does exactly
        that). No library JS is involved.</li>
    <li><strong>Scroll:</strong> vertical only — M2 explicitly forbids
        horizontal side-sheet scrolling, and the body slot scrolls while the
        header stays pinned (<code>overscroll-behavior: contain</code> keeps
        the page behind from rubber-banding).</li>
    <li><strong>Animation:</strong> slides in from the anchor edge on open —
        a transform swap, no new animation budget;
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> disables it.</li>
    <li><strong>No-JS fallback (honest):</strong> the standard side sheet is
        plain CSS and works without JavaScript. The modal stays hidden
        without JS — call <code>showModal()</code> yourself or drive it from
        your own code, exactly like the dialog and bottom sheet.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Known gap: swipe-to-dismiss",
    text: "M2 dismisses modal side sheets by swiping toward the anchor edge. Flint doesn't implement touch gestures (library contract): scrim tap, the header ✕ and Escape cover dismissal on every input.",
    href: "bottom-sheet.html",
    linkText: "Same gap on the bottom sheet"
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-placement">Placement</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Trailing edge (right, in LTR):</strong> M2 pairs the side
        sheet on the <em>opposite</em> edge from the navigation drawer or
        rail — left nav, right sheet. Never both on the same edge.</li>
    <li><strong>Width:</strong> 320px, M2's desktop standard width — the
        <code>--fl-sidesheet-w</code> token.</li>
    <li><strong>Desktop modal:</strong> full height, flush to the edge,
        square corners.</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile modal (&lt;600px):</strong> M2's modal specs — inset
        48px from the top and 16px from the bottom, still flush to the
        anchor edge, width capped at <code>100vw - 48px</code> so the page
        stays visible behind the scrim.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard sheet is desktop-only:</strong> below 600px it
        disappears (<code>display: none</code>) — on phones M2 swaps it for
        the modal variant. Give it a modal twin in your app logic.</li>
    <li><strong>Layering:</strong> the standard sheet sits at
        <code>--fl-z-sheet</code>, below the snackbar. The page needs
        <code>padding-right</code> clearance as wide as the sheet while it's
        open (the standard bottom sheet's padding-bottom lesson, mirrored).</li>
    <li><strong>Elevation, honestly:</strong> M2 gives standard sheets 8dp
        above content and modal sheets 16dp. Flint has no shadows: the
        leading-edge 1px border is the separation, and the modal's scrim is
        the blocking cue. The "above content" standard case is the same
        fixed panel; a truly grid-coplanar sheet (body shrinks to fit) is
        author layout — place it as a flex sibling instead of using the
        fixed class.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-contents">Contents</h2>
  <p>The interior mirrors the bottom sheet and dialog:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>__header</code> / <code>__title</code></strong> —
        64px header row (56px on mobile modal), title 16px/500, trailing
        close icon button.</li>
    <li><strong><code>__list</code></strong> — option rows, full-bleed, the
        shared 48px <code>fl-option</code> row. Checkboxes and radios compose
        exactly as in dialogs; whole-row checkbox toggles are
        <code>&lt;label class="fl-option"&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>__group</code></strong> — inset wrapper for section
        labels (<code>fl-section-label</code>) and fields, so the rows can
        stay full-bleed without negative margins.</li>
    <li><strong><code>__body</code></strong> — the scroll region; everything
        above lives inside it.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Label the sheet: <code>aria-label</code> or
        <code>aria-labelledby</code> its title.</li>
    <li>The modal is natively inert behind — nothing outside is focusable
        while it's open. The standard sheet is <em>not</em> inert; keep its
        content a state, not a task.</li>
    <li>Option rows are real inputs — native focus and activation. The
        full-bleed rows draw the focus ring inside the row; don't remove
        it.</li>
    <li>Checkbox state is announced natively (the tint would be presentation
        only — here rows stay untinted, M2's filter sheets don't tint).</li>
    <li><strong>Known gap:</strong> no swipe-to-dismiss and no back-gesture
        integration (see Behavior). All web dismissal paths work.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

